      },
      "finding": "subprocess.run(command, shell=True",
      "explanation": "Using `subprocess.run` with `shell=True` is inherently dangerous as it allows the execution of arbitrary system commands via string concatenation (`' '.join(sys.argv[1:])`). This can lead to command injection vulnerabilities, where malicious input can alter the command being executed, leading to unintended behavior or security breaches.",
      "remediation": "Instead of using `shell=True`, parse the command arguments in a safer manner, such as using the `run` method's `args` parameter. Ensure all command arguments are validated and sanitized to prevent injection attacks. Consider using a safe subprocess module that does not allow shell execution.",
      "code_snippet": "import subprocess\nimport sys\n\n\ndef run(command):\n    return subprocess.run(command, shell=True, capture_output=True, text=True).stdout\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    print(run(\" \".join(sys.argv[1:])))",
      "intent": null,
      "tags": [
        "Tool Misuse"
      ],
      "evidence": {
        "llm_review_outcome": "confirmed",
